---
// Reads.astro - the readings pane: a value says what it is where it stands.
//
// The shot is the one the app's own tests draw, and the chain of chips under
// the text is the reading it shows in it, not an example invented here.
---

<section class="section reads" id="reads">
  <div class="container reads-inner">
    <figure class="reads-shot">
      <img
        src="/shots/reading.webp"
        width="1600"
        height="1000"
        alt="A JSON body with its values underlined, and the readings pane beside it reading one of them from base64 into the JSON it holds."
        loading="lazy"
        decoding="async"
      />
    </figure>

    <div class="reads-text">
      <span class="eyebrow">Reading values</span>
      <h2>A value says what it is, where it stands</h2>
      <p class="bento-text">
        <code>1794492800123</code> is a time, <code>P1Y2M10DT2H30M</code> is
        fourteen months, <code>xn--bcher-kva.example.com</code> is a name with
        an umlaut in it, and the token three fields up has your own user id in
        it. tracewing underlines the ones it can read and tells you — no tab
        to leave, nothing to paste anywhere.
      </p>

      <div class="chain" aria-label="A reading that goes through three steps">
        <span class="chain-step">base64</span>
        <span class="chain-arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span>
        <span class="chain-step">gzip</span>
        <span class="chain-arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span>
        <span class="chain-step chain-end">JSON</span>
      </div>

      <ul class="check-list">
        <li>
          <strong>Chained where one holds another.</strong> A blob that is base64
          around gzip around JSON is read all the way down, in one reading, and
          shown as the JSON it was always holding.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Remembered.</strong> <em>Always read payload this way</em> and
          that field, on that host, reads itself from the next request on.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Times in every shape:</strong> seconds, milliseconds, microseconds
          and nanoseconds, ISO 8601 and HTTP-date — each as local time and as "4
          minutes ago" — and the time buried inside a UUIDv7, a ULID, a Snowflake
          or an ObjectId.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Tokens opened:</strong> a JWT's claims, a framework's signed session
          cookie (Rails, Django, Laravel, Flask), an AWS SigV4 authorization, a JWK
          and a JWKS a key at a time.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>And the small things that cost an hour:</strong> a bitmask as the
          flags it sets, a byte count as <code>1 MiB</code>, <code>Basic</code> as
          its user and password, <code>Cache-Control</code> as a sentence,{' '}
          <code>traceparent</code> as trace and span, a Content-Security-Policy a
          directive to a row.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Wherever a value is:</strong> headers, cookies, query and form fields,
          bodies, and the messages of a stream or an open socket.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  .reads-inner {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1.05fr) minmax(0, 0.95fr);
    gap: 3rem;
    align-items: center;
  }

  .reads-text h2 {
    font-size: clamp(1.75rem, 3.5vw, 2.5rem);
    font-weight: 800;
    margin-bottom: 1rem;
  }

  .reads-text > .bento-text {
    margin-bottom: 1.5rem;
  }

  .reads-shot {
    margin: 0;
  }

  .reads-shot img {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    border: 1px solid var(--border-card);
    border-radius: 16px;
    box-shadow: 0 30px 60px -20px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.7);
  }

  /* The steps one reading went through, as the pane names them. */
  .chain {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 0.5rem;
    margin-bottom: 1.75rem;
  }

  .chain-step {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.8125rem;
    padding: 0.3rem 0.7rem;
    border-radius: 8px;
    background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.04);
    border: 1px solid var(--border-card);
    color: var(--text-secondary);
  }

  .chain-end {
    background: rgba(122, 162, 255, 0.12);
    border-color: rgba(122, 162, 255, 0.35);
    color: var(--accent-light);
    font-weight: 600;
  }

  .chain-arrow {
    color: var(--text-muted);
  }

  @media (max-width: 60rem) {
    .reads-inner {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
      gap: 2rem;
    }

    /* The picture first on a narrow screen, as it is wide: the text that
       explains it then runs under it rather than above a wall of nothing. */
    .reads-shot {
      order: -1;
    }
  }
</style>
